# TON whose PT changes while the timer is running.
#
# What the standard says: TON is defined by a timing diagram (ed.3 table
# 46) in which PT is constant. The diagram says nothing about a PT that
# changes after the rising edge of IN, neither whether the new PT is used
# immediately, nor whether Q may fall again when PT is raised above the
# elapsed time. Implementations differ (some latch PT at the rising edge,
# some compare against the live PT every evaluation).
#
# What IronPLC 0.251.0 (5ea0610) does (vm/src/intrinsic.rs, `ton`): the
# live PT is used on every evaluation for the comparison and for clamping
# ET; so lowering PT below the elapsed time makes Q rise at once (cycle 3).
# But once Q is TRUE it is only cleared when IN goes FALSE: raising PT to
# 800 ms at cycle 5 leaves Q TRUE while ET climbs from 400 to 800 ms
# (cycles 5 to 9), i.e. Q = TRUE with ET < PT. Recorded 2026-10-31.
period_ms: 100
program: main
inputs: in pt
outputs: q et
status: implementation-defined
reference: IEC 61131-3 ed.3 6.6.3.5.4 table 46 (TON timing diagram)
---
0 in=FALSE pt=T#500ms => q=FALSE et=T#0ms
# rising edge at t = 100
1 in=TRUE => q=FALSE et=T#0ms
2 => q=FALSE et=T#100ms
# PT lowered to 200 ms: elapsed 200 >= new PT, Q rises immediately
3 pt=T#200ms => q=TRUE et=T#200ms
4 => q=TRUE et=T#200ms
# PT raised to 800 ms: Q stays TRUE although ET < PT
5 pt=T#800ms => q=TRUE et=T#400ms
6 => q=TRUE et=T#500ms
7 => q=TRUE et=T#600ms
8 => q=TRUE et=T#700ms
9 => q=TRUE et=T#800ms
10 => q=TRUE et=T#800ms
11 => q=TRUE et=T#800ms
12 in=FALSE => q=FALSE et=T#0ms
